<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baldwin II of Jerusalem</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1131</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Baldwin of Bourcq</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Baldwin II, Baldwin of Bourcq, was Count of Edessa from 1100 and King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> from 1118 to 1131. As count he was captured by the Turks at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a> in 1104 and held for several years. As king he also acted as regent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, was again a prisoner in 1123–1124, and saw the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> in 1124.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baldwin-ii-of-jerusalem.jpg" alt="Baldwin II of Jerusalem" width="210" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_Baldwin2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Baldwin's birth year is unknown. He was the son of Count Hugh I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rethel">Rethel</a> and Melisende of Montlhéry, probably a younger child, and was a kinsman of the brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-of-bouillon">Godfrey of Bouillon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-i-of-jerusalem">Baldwin of Boulogne</a>. His family spoke the Champenois dialect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-french">Old French</a>, and he was probably illiterate. As lord of Bourcq he joined Godfrey's army at the beginning of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>. William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> describes him as very religious, with callouses on his hands and knees from frequent prayer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-i-of-jerusalem">Baldwin of Boulogne</a> granted him the County of Edessa when he left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> on 2 October 1100. Baldwin married Morphia, the daughter of the Armenian lord Gabriel of Melitene, which strengthened his position among his largely Armenian subjects. At the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a> on 7 May 1104 Sökmen and Jikirmish drew the crusaders into an ambush, and he was captured; Tancred governed Edessa as regent. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joscelin-i-count-of-edessa">Joscelin of Courtenay</a> ransomed him in 1108, and Tancred gave back the county only after the prelates arbitrated in Baldwin's favour. He returned to Edessa on 18 September 1108.</p><p>Mawdud besieged Edessa in April 1110, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-i-of-jerusalem">Baldwin I of Jerusalem</a> came to relieve it. The childless Baldwin I died on 2 April 1118, and Baldwin of Bourcq was elected king by the nobles. After the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> was almost destroyed in June 1119 he became regent for Bohemond II of Antioch, and his time in the north caused discontent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Belek captured the king on 18 April 1123 while he prepared to hunt with falcons, and held him at Kharput, later at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>. Jerusalem's regents captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> on 7 or 8 July 1124. Baldwin was released in August 1124, but al-Bursuqi forced him to lift the siege of Aleppo in January 1125. In 1126 Bohemond II married his daughter Alice, and in 1129 his eldest daughter Melisende married Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulk-king-of-jerusalem">Fulk V of Anjou</a>. He took monastic vows and died on 21 August 1131; Melisende and Fulk were crowned on 14 September. William of Tyre called him devout and experienced in war, but others criticised his love of money.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Baldwin II of Jerusalem” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nur ad-Din's madrasa and hospital in Damascus and his mosque at Mosul remained in use, and his fortification of the citadel of Aleppo, where Joscelin II of Edessa and <b>Baldwin II of Jerusalem</b> were held, shaped one of the great castles of the Levant.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zengid-dynasty">Zengid dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its most famous holder was Belek Ghazi, a nephew of Ilghazi, who captured Joscelin I of the County of Edessa in 1122 and King <b>Baldwin II of Jerusalem</b> in 1123 and imprisoned them there.</blockquote><a href="/state/artuqids-of-harput">Artuqids of Harput</a></li><li><blockquote>Its counts, <b>Baldwin II</b>, Joscelin I and Joscelin II, were exposed on every side; the defeat at Harran in 1104 halted their advance towards Mesopotamia.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-edessa">County of Edessa</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rethel">Rethel</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Jerusalem, in what is now <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.51,4.37&amp;year=1121" aria-label="Rethel on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4650 439 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Baldwin II of Jerusalem"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5100 709h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5100 709h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.51,4.37&amp;year=1121">What was here in 1121? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
